  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (through next Wednesday)...\n\nConvective activity in our east this afternoon is expected to\nshift into the Dakotas by evening. Heavy rain is our main concern\nwith these storms, but wet microbursts with gusts to 65 mph are\nalso possible. Out west, weak forcing and a drying environment\nwill limit convection to weak isolated cells. Look for highs\ntopping out in the mid 90s this afternoon. \n\nLow level moisture will decrease tonight into Friday with PWATs \ndropping to under an inch across the area. Models suggest a weak \nshortwave tracking over the area tonight which may produce an area\nof showers and isolated thundershowers overnight into early \nFriday across east central Montana, before drifting into our \nsoutheast by midday Friday and maybe into the afternoon. \nOtherwise, convective activity Friday looks minimal. Look for high\ntemps 90s to near 100F Friday.\n\nSignificant 700mb thermal ridge exerts itself Saturday. 700b \ntemps ranging from 16-20C into our region suggest surface temps \nover 100F. The chance of at least 105F is highest along the river\nvalleys from Billings to Miles City (45-60%). The extreme heat \nwill be a health risk for folks outdoors. We have continued an \nExtreme Heat Watch across our lower elevations from noon to \nmidnight. As an upper trough pushes into the Pacific NW and \nnorthern Rockies late Saturday into Sunday, we will see the upper \nridge suppressed. An associated cold front will push thru Saturday\nnight. Thus, Sunday will be cooler, but still hot/dry, with \nbreezy north to northeast winds over the east. A stronger frontal \npassage arrives Sunday night setting the stage for notably cooler \ndays Monday & Tuesday with highs in the 70s for some locations,\nalong with breezy conditions. Low amplitude ridge comes back by\nmidweek, and should push temps back to around 90F Wednesday.\n\nA few light showers are possible Monday as upper trough axis \nshifts across the region. Otherwise dry conditions will prevail \nthrough the middle of next week. \n\nFire Weather: The triple digit heat and 10% or less humidity will\nelevate fire weather concerns Saturday. Mixed winds will be only \nsomewhat breezy during the peak heating hours, but with the \nextreme temps and possibly single digit RH values, we have opted \nto issue a Fire Weather Watch from Yellowstone County westward. We\nwill go thru Sunday with this as temps will still be in the the \n90s and RH still in the teens Sunday afternoon, we may see some \nafternoon winds approach marginal criteria. Valid period is 2 pm \nSaturday to 9 pm Sunday. \n\nSmoke impacts: Post-frontal subsidence and shift to upslope winds\nmay yield higher concentrations of surface-based smoke for the\nlatter half of the weekend into Monday. Reduced air quality is \npossible. Something to monitor over the next few days.\n\nClimate tidbits: Smoke permitting, there is a very good chance \nthat daily records will be broken this Saturday August 1st. \nBillings, Livingston & Sheridan may get close monthly (August) \nrecords as well. These are summarized below.\n\nSITE      8/1 FORECAST   8/1 RECORD      AUG RECORD\nBillings     105         102 (2008)    105 (8/2/2024)\nLivingston   104         101 (2000)    105 (8/5/1961)\nMiles City   105         104 (2008)    110 (8/7/1995)\nSheridan     106         103 (2008)    106 (8/7/1983)\nBaker        102          99 (2022)    108 (8/12/2018)\n\nBT",
